's stomach: not just money, but the machinery of the theater district's misery, oiled and humming."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Squeeze', 'text': "Ruby's club was next — a fire in the kitchen, a message in the smoke. Eddie Cross found her unharmed and furious, and that fury was worth ten guns. 'End this,' she said. Eddie Cross intended to.\n\nThe dame vanished. Of course she did. Eddie Cross found her scarf in the office, red as a warning, and underneath it a note: 'Forgive me. They have my brother.' So that was the price. Everyone has one.\n\nthe fixer squeezed back. First the office: tossed, the safe cracked, a broken watch's copy gone — but Eddie Cross keeps the originals where no one looks. Then Ruby the singer: a warning delivered with fists in an alley off the theater district."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Confrontation', 'text': "The dame got her brother back and Eddie Cross got the rest of the fee, which was more than the insulting original. She cried. Eddie Cross didn't buy the tears but didn't charge for the shoulder, either.\n\nEddie Cross walked into the fixer's office at noon, when the theater district was bright and honest-looking, which made the lies inside seem darker. a broken watch went on the desk. The photographs went on the desk. The truth went on the desk.\n\nIt went down the way it always goes: shouting, threats, a gun that Eddie Cross was faster than. When the police took the fixer out, the theater district seemed to exhale, like a drunk finally telling the truth."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Last Call', 'text': "the theater district kept being the theater district: rain, neon, lies, and now and then — now and then — a little justice, served neat, no ice. Eddie Cross could live with that. Eddie Cross had lived with worse.\n\nLou poured a double on the house. Ruby sang the song she only sings when she means it. Ruby the singer's ribs were taped, but Ruby the singer drank anyway, doctor's orders being for other people.\n\nThe dame left town on the morning train, brother in tow, free and clean. She left Eddie Cross a note: 'You were the only honest thing in it.' Eddie Cross kept the note. Detectives keep everything."}]}

