ixer to a warehouse on the waterfront. What they saw there turned Frank Delaney's stomach: not just money, but the machinery of Grand Central Station's misery, oiled and humming."}, {'n': 4, 'title': 'The Squeeze', 'text': "Frank Delaney sat up all night with a one-way ticket and the photographs and the whiskey going untouched. By dawn there was a plan. It wasn't a good plan. It was the only plan.\n\nThe dame vanished. Of course she did. Frank Delaney found her scarf in the office, red as a warning, and underneath it a note: 'Forgive me. They have my brother.' So that was the price. Everyone has one.\n\nRuby's club was next — a fire in the kitchen, a message in the smoke. Frank Delaney found her unharmed and furious, and that fury was worth ten guns. 'End this,' she said. Frank Delaney intended to."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Confrontation', 'text': "That night it rained again, washing Grand Central Station clean for an hour. Frank Delaney stood at the window with a one-way ticket — the case closed, the ledger in the DA's hands. loyalty in a dirty town, Frank Delaney thought. Even here. Especially here.\n\nIt went down the way it always goes: shouting, threats, a gun that Frank Delaney was faster than. When the police took Mr. Big out, Grand Central Station seemed to exhale, like a drunk finally telling the truth.\n\nThe dame got her brother back and Frank Delaney got the rest of the fee, which was more than the insulting original. She cried. Frank Delaney didn't buy the tears but didn't charge for the shoulder, either."}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Last Call', 'text': "The dame left town on the morning train, brother in tow, free and clean. She left Frank Delaney a note: 'You were the only honest thing in it.' Frank Delaney kept the note. Detectives keep everything.\n\nGrand Central Station kept being Grand Central Station: rain, neon, lies, and now and then — now and then — a little justice, served neat, no ice. Frank Delaney could live with that. Frank Delaney had lived wi